  <section class="chapter wrap" id="load">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 8 · When the table fills up</p>
      <h2>Load factor and rehashing</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>The more keys a table holds, the more collisions there are, and the more tries each insert and search needs. To know when a table is getting too crowded, we measure how full it is.</p>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Load factor</p>
        <p>The <b>load factor</b> is <b>α = n / m</b> ("alpha"): n is the number of keys stored, and m is the number of slots. Our lesson table has 6 keys in 7 slots: α = 6/7 ≈ 0.86.</p>
      </div>
      <p>It means slightly different things for the two families:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Separate chaining:</b> α is the average chain length. It can go above 1 (more keys than buckets), but long chains are slow.</li>
        <li><b>Open addressing:</b> α can never go above 1, because each slot holds one key. At α = 1 the table is full.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For linear probing in a big table, here is about how many tries a search takes on average (from the formulas in Sedgewick and Wayne's <i>Algorithms</i>):</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Load factor α</th><th>Search, key is there</th><th>Search, key is not there</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">0.5 (half full)</td><td>1.5 tries</td><td>2.5 tries</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">0.75</td><td>2.5 tries</td><td>8.5 tries</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">0.9</td><td>5.5 tries</td><td>50.5 tries</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>At half full, it is still about one or two tries. Near full, it gets slow fast. So tables are kept from getting too full: open addressing usually aims for α ≤ 0.5, and Java's <code>HashMap</code> (chaining) grows when α passes 0.75.</p>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Rehashing</p>
        <p><b>Rehashing</b> means making a bigger table (often about twice as big, and a prime) and inserting <b>every key again</b> with the new m.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Why not just copy the keys over to the same slot numbers? Because every slot number came from <b>k mod m</b>, and m has changed. In the bigger table, the keys belong in new places:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={rehashPic()} /><figcaption>Rehashing 4 keys from m = 5 into m = 11 (linear probing in both).</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Rehashing takes work (every key is inserted again), but it happens rarely: after doubling, many more inserts fit before the next one. So, on average, inserts stay fast.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="speed">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 9 · Why this matters</p>
      <h2>How fast is a hash table?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Here is how finding one key compares, for n keys:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Where the keys are</th><th>Find, usually</th><th>Find, worst case</th><th>Keeps keys in order?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Unsorted array (linear search)</td><td>up to n checks</td><td>n</td><td>no</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sorted array (binary search)</td><td>about log₂ n (20 for a million)</td><td>about log₂ n</td><td>yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Balanced BST (AVL, red-black)</td><td>about log₂ n</td><td>about log₂ n</td><td>yes (in-order)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Hash table</b></td><td><b>1 or 2 tries</b>, if the load factor is kept low</td><td>n</td><td><b>no</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>The worst case happens when (almost) every key collides: one long chain, or one long cluster. Remember <Peek label="the prices in a table of 10" title="From Part 2" html={pricesPeek} />. A good hash function, a prime m, and rehashing keep that from happening in practice.</p>
      <p>Notice the last column: a hash table scatters the keys on purpose, so it cannot list them in order, or quickly find the smallest. Here is how to choose:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>You need to...</th><th>Good choice</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>find, add, and remove by exact key, many times (ID → student record, username → account)</td><td>hash table (<code>HashMap</code>, <code>HashSet</code>)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>keep keys sorted, find the smallest or largest, or list a range (all grades from 80 to 90)</td><td>balanced BST (<code>TreeMap</code>, <code>TreeSet</code>)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>store a few values, or values by position</td><td>array or list</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Java's own <code>HashMap</code> and <code>HashSet</code> use <b>separate chaining</b> (see <Peek label="the chaining picture in Part 4" title="From Part 4" html={chainPeek} />), and double their table size when the load factor passes 0.75.</p>
      <p class="small muted">Good to know: books write the hash table's usual speed as <b>O(1)</b> on average ("constant": it does not grow with n), and its worst case as <b>O(n)</b>. You will meet these names again when the course analyzes algorithms.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

